Falsifies `system_tests::user::receive_cap_attack_tests::a_plain_send_to_receive_cap_delivers_no_cap_whichever_side_parks_first`.

Architecture: all three (the change is in shared scheduler code, `kernel/src/sched.rs`).

The defect this restores was live on `main` until milestone 634 (a plain SEND received by RECEIVE_CAP never hands the receiver a sender-chosen slot). `ipc_receive_cap`'s receiver-first mailbox read returned
the parked mailbox unchanged, so a plain `SEND` that reached a parked `RECEIVE_CAP` receiver left the
sender's second word in `x1` where a `CALL` server reads a reply slot. This patch reverts the one
hunk that makes the receive side write `NO_CAP` unless a capability was installed, putting the leak
back. The hunk's context cites milestone 151 (notification objects: async multiplexing without
wait-any), whose bound-notification tag the fix exempts.

Expected to fail: the receiver-first assertion
`assert_eq!(RECEIVER_FIRST_X1, abi::rendezvous::NO_CAP, ...)`, because the sender's chosen word (7)
is delivered in `x1` again. The sender-first half stays green (it never went through this path),
which is what gives the red its meaning: the two orders are asserted together so the fix's symmetry
is the property, and only the order the defect touched turns red.

Re-cut on 2026-10-03 (UTC) by the lane for milestone 706 (a `CALL` server can tell a Reply from a
delegation), whose comment above the removed lines moved the context, and replayed red at the same
assertion on aarch64.

Regenerated 2026-10-11 (UTC) for milestone 840 (the scheduler file is split along its seams):
the same lines, moved out of `kernel/src/sched.rs` into `kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs` unchanged, so the defect is
the same and only the path and line numbers differ.

diff --git a/kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs b/kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs
index c59bccd96..dfd1371fa 100644
--- a/kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs
@@ -712,17 +712,7 @@ pub fn ipc_receive_cap(ep: RendezvousId) -> [u64; 5] {
             // receive, `abi::rendezvous::REPLY_DELIVERED` when a CALL's Reply was installed
             // (milestone 706, written by `ipc_call_badged`), and `0` for every other delivery a
             // sender or the kernel's death path makes (milestone 151).
-            // **x1 is NO_CAP unless a capability was installed for this delivery** (milestone 634,
-            // fatal risk 7). A plain SEND that reached us parked drops its three words straight into
-            // the mailbox, so m[1] is the sender's chosen word; returning it would hand a sender a
-            // slot number where a CALL server reads a reply slot. A bound-notification delivery is
-            // exempt: its x4 == BOUND and its m[1] is the notification word, not a sender's choice.
-            let m = t.mailbox;
-            if t.capability_delivered || m[4] == abi::notification::BOUND {
-                m
-            } else {
-                [m[0], NO_CAP, m[1], m[3], 0]
-            }
+            t.mailbox
         }
     }
 }
